result:
Fixed and resubmitted.

**Defect 1.1 — accepted, not disputed.** I verified it independently rather than taking it on trust: `head -1` across the siblings confirms `p1/answer.tex` and `p2/answer.tex` both open with `\section*{...}`, so my `\subsection*` was the only one of the three. Assembled in order, Ex. 2.4 would have been demoted a level and read as a sub-part of Ex. 2.2 — a real assembly bug, not a style nit.

The fix is line 1 only: `\subsection*` → `\section*`. No prose was touched, per your explicit instruction not to shorten.

Re-verified after the change:
- recompiled through a wrapper that `\input`s the fragment — 1 page, 0 errors, no extra packages needed;
- re-rendered and **read the page back**: the heading now sets at section weight (wrapping over two lines), body unchanged and clean, no overfull lines;
- body word count still **212**, confirming the prose is byte-identical;
- tightened my own check to assert the file starts with `\section*{` and that the string `subsection` appears nowhere in it — both pass, so this specific regression can't recur silently;
- `lint_output.py` exits 0 with `answer.tex` as the only file in OUTPUT.

`submission.md` now carries a round-2 section documenting the defect and the re-verification, with the round-1 record preserved below it; I also corrected the one stale `\subsection*` reference in the round-1 text.
